For the most part, partial derivatives are quite easy to compute, once you become adept at treating variables like constants. 
If 

	f(x, y) = x²y + cos(x + y), 

then we have 

	(δf)/(δx) = 2xy - sin(x + y). 

(Imagine y to be constant throughtout the differentiation process)
Also 

	(δf)/(δy) = x² - sin(x + y), 

(Imagine x to be a constant)
Similarly, if g(x, y) = (xy)/(x² + y²), then, from the quotient rule of ordinary calculus, we have 

	g(sub(x))(x, y) = ((x² + y²)y - xy(2x))/(x² + y²)² = (x(x² - y²))/(x² + y²)²

Note that, of course, neither g nor its partial derivatives are defined at (0,0)
